In article <97@rc6.urc.tue.nl> wsbucan@eutws1.win.tue.nl (Boudewijn Wayers) writes:
>I don't think one should be able to recharge magic lamps.
>Recharging these lamps is illogic, since the "charge" is in fact
>a djinny (SP?), that is released when the lamp is rubbed. Since
>the djinny gives you a wish, and not the lamp, a magic lamp should
>be one-use only.

I sort of semi-answered this in one AD&D adventure that I DM'ed.  The party
ran into this iron flask with all sorts of seals and stuff on it, so after
they made suitable preparations they nuked the seals and popped the cork.
Out popped... not a djinni, but a custom-made creature that might have well
have been a djinni.

In the ensuing discussion, they asked him whether he enjoyed living in such
a small bottle.  Of course, he answered, "Live in a bottle?  What a silly
idea!  I live in a huge mansion on the plane of fire."  After a little more
discussion, the party gets out of him the fact that this iron bottle is
just a portal that he created so that he could travel from his plane to this
plane.  A wizard on this end then decided that he didn't want the djinni
using it to travel, and sealed it up, at the same time magicking it so that
if the seal was broken the djinni would be involuntarily summoned.

Thus, a scroll of charging would (in this case) re-seal the bottle and 
replenish the summoning magic.  Thus, perhaps to add to realism, it would
make sense to have the lamp move towards being cursed after being used,
and not be able to be blessed.  In other words, a djinni who was summoned
once might be willing to grant a wish, because he now has his portal back,
but upon being summoned again, he'd be much less disposed to give a wish.

